  derived_from _aterm:Change_Impact_Review_Procedure_
  derived_from _aterm:Migration_Planning_Guidance_
  derived_from _aterm:Corpus_Inspection_Guidance_
  derived_from _aterm:Semantic_Review_Guidance_
  derived_from _aterm:Environment_
  derived_from _aterm:Target_Context_
  derived_from _aterm:Change_Set_
  derived_from _aterm:Corpus_Inspection_Guidance_
  derived_from _aterm:GraphQL_Query_
  derived_from _aterm:Command_Invocation_Guide_
  derived_from _aterm:Corpus_Query_Procedure_
  derived_from _aterm:Corpus_Notation_Guide_
  derived_from _aterm:Relation_
  derived_from _aterm:Search_Space_
  derived_from _aterm:Change_Set_
  references _Runtime_Management_Skill_
.description
  Use to read Terms, search names or text, discover concepts from intent, or analyze change impact with CLI or GraphQL.
.body
  - Choose the query or comparison that answers the question. A focused lookup need not become an exhaustive audit; state limits when claiming coverage.

  ## Select the question first

  - Use a name query for canonical spellings and a text query for literal content.

  ```sh
  aterm term list '*Task*'
  aterm corpus search 'completion'
  ```

  - Use discovery for an intent; use graph relations and occurrences for dependency effects.

  ```sh
  aterm corpus discover 'reopen completed work' --mode lexical
  aterm graph relations _vending_machine:Slot_
  aterm term occurrences _vending_machine:Slot_
  ```

  - Names, globs, section keys and literal text ignore case by default; output retains canonical spelling.

  ```sh
  aterm term list '*task*'
  aterm corpus search 'Completion' --case-sensitive
  aterm term view _inventory:Stock_Item_ --section DEFINITION
  ```

  - Use --case-sensitive when exact case matters or case-only Term names are ambiguous.
  - Preserve exact returned names for authored references and writes; read matching never changes identity.
  - Quote globs; record the selected Home, Knowledge, Viewpoints, relation filters, case mode and external sources.
  - An empty result is evidence only about that examined selection.

  > No matches in one filtered Knowledge, therefore no such concept exists — BAD.
  >
  > No matches in the named Knowledge under the recorded filters — GOOD.

  - Read the complete owner before treating a hit or section excerpt as a contract.
  - For authoring, read writer Scope explicitly; reader Description is not its substitute.

  ```sh
  aterm term view _vending_machine:Slot_ --detail
  ```

  - A Term has one Term Declaration with one Term Kind; that declaration owns its sections and outgoing declarations.
  - A reference mentions another Term; a Relation asserts a connection; neither performs an action.
  - has_state lists permitted States, not the active State. Follow incoming derived_from edges to find affected explanations.

  ## Project only the necessary fields

  - Select a small GraphQL projection when the task needs an inventory or a bounded traversal.

  ```sh
  aterm corpus query '{ knowledges { nodes { id description } pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor } } }'
  aterm corpus query '{ __type(name: "Term") { fields { name } } }'
  ```

  > Fetch every Term Declaration to list Knowledge names — BAD.
  >
  > Select Knowledge id and description — GOOD.

  - MCP uses `graphql({query: "{ knowledges { nodes { id description } pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor } } }"})`.
  - Traverse term.outgoing with target, or term.incoming with source; choose phrase, origin and structural filters deliberately.
  - GraphQL selector fields accept caseSensitive: true; CLI corpus query --case-sensitive supplies their default. A field override affects only that field.
  - origin relation selects declarations; origin reference selects body references. Viewpoint and phrase filters accept quoted globs.
  - `termDeclarations(termKind: "specification.concept")` returns the single Term Declaration if its Term Kind matches; omission returns that Term Declaration without filtering.
  - Read exact section content with `sections(keys: ["definition"]) { key format content }`; JSON/YAML bodies remain strings.
  - Follow pageInfo.hasNextPage with after: endCursor. first defaults to 20 and accepts 1–100.
  - On a stale cursor, restart the collection; never combine pages from different corpus versions.
  - Inspect GraphQL errors even after HTTP 200; CLI errors exit 1 and MCP errors set isError.
  - A projection is an excerpt: read full owners and required context before asserting complete obligations.
  - GraphQL does not write or rank semantic matches; use authoring commands or corpus discover.

  ## Filter and sort typed GraphQL collections

  - Use terms, relations, knowledges, viewpoints or termKinds as the root for a typed query.
  - All five collections return nodes, totalCount and pageInfo, including nested viewpoints and termKinds.
  - where receives one candidate object and must return one boolean; do not start with .[].
  - Term objects use @term, @knowledge, @termKind and parsed section fields, as in corpus jq.

  ```graphql
  {
    terms(knowledge: "japan_trip", termKind: "trip.transport",
      where: ".schedule.date == \"2026-10-28\" and .booking.status != \"confirmed\"",
      orderBy: [{key: ".schedule.date"}, {key: ".schedule.departure"}], first: 20) {
      nodes { id termDeclarations { definition sections(keys: ["schedule"]) { key content } } }
      totalCount
      pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
    }
  }
  ```

  - Field names and status values above are examples; inspect the selected Viewpoint and actual sections first.
  - Missing status matches != "confirmed"; add .booking.status != null to exclude missing status.
  - Each orderBy key must emit one scalar or null; direction is ASC by default or DESC, nulls is LAST by default or FIRST.
  - Multiple keys sort in order; ties retain default order. Filter and sort precede pagination.
  - Relations expose source and target Term records to predicates; e.g. .target.schedule.date == "2026-10-28".
  - Knowledge predicates use .id; Viewpoint predicates use .name and .knowledge.id; Term Kind predicates use .name, .qualifiedName and .viewpoint.knowledge.id.
  - Root Viewpoints and Term Kinds are bindings per Knowledge; identical names across Knowledges are not merged.
  - A request permits four jq evaluations total, counting root jq and each collection using where/orderBy.
  - Use root jq for transformations or aggregation; typed collections preserve GraphQL field selection and traversal.

  ## Pass reusable parameters

  - Supply GraphQL variables separately from the document; a variable inside a quoted jq expression is not substituted by GraphQL.
  - Use bindings on the field that evaluates jq to pass typed JSON values; do not build a predicate by interpolating user text.

  ```graphql
  query TripDate($knowledge: ID!, $date: String!, $after: String) {
    terms(knowledge: $knowledge, where: ".schedule.date == $date",
      bindings: {date: $date}, first: 20, after: $after) {
      nodes { id termDeclarations { definition } }
      pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
    }
  }
  ```

  ```json
  {"knowledge": "japan_trip", "date": "2026-10-28"}
  ```

  - Run the saved document with a variables object, or send the same query/variables envelope through MCP graphql or POST /graphql.

  ```sh
  aterm corpus query --file trip-date.graphql --variables '{"knowledge":"japan_trip","date":"2026-10-28"}'
  ```

  - bindings also works with orderBy and root jq; nested fields need their own bindings.
  - Binding names match [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*; the serialized object must fit 64 KiB. Null or omission supplies no bindings.
  - Omit a GraphQL variable to use its query default; explicit null is a value and is invalid for a non-null variable.
  - Keep parameters unchanged while paging; after changing a binding, restart without the old cursor.
  - For connected Terms, filter relations on .source or .target section values and select source { id } and target { id }; direct connections are not recursive closure.

  > A date-matched Term plus one returned Relation proves the whole day's model was read — BAD.
  >
  > State the date predicate, direct-connection boundary and any remaining pages — GOOD.

  ## Query section values with jq

  - Use corpus jq for text conditions, JSON/YAML fields and cross-Knowledge aggregation.

  ```sh
  aterm corpus jq '.[] | select(.definition | contains("payment")) | .["@term"]'
  aterm corpus jq '.[] | select(.schedule.stay_booking_status == "confirmed") | {term: .["@term"], date: .schedule.date}' --knowledges japan_trip
  ```

  - Input is one array of Terms; @term and @knowledge identify each record, and sections are its other properties.
  - JSON/YAML sections become values; other sections remain text. Missing sections are absent.
  - Use optional access for sparse nested data and grouping for aggregate questions.

  ```sh
  aterm corpus jq '.[] | select(any(.schedule.draft_plan.events[]?; .kind == "meal")) | .["@term"]' --knowledges japan_trip
  aterm corpus jq 'group_by(.["@knowledge"]) | map({knowledge: .[0]["@knowledge"], count: length})'
  ```

  - jq keeps its own case rules; use `test("payment"; "i")` for insensitive text matching.
  - GraphQL exposes the same evaluator, including the knowledge array.

  ```graphql
  { jq(knowledge: ["japan_trip"], program: ".[] | select(.schedule.stay_booking_status == \"confirmed\") | .[\"@term\"]") }
  ```

  - Output is the array of jq emissions: empty emits [], null emits [null], and map emits one nested array.
  - Omit knowledge to read the whole admitted corpus; [] selects no Terms. This input is not paginated.
  - Execution is bounded: 64 KiB program, 16 MiB input, 2 MiB output and 5 seconds. Select fewer Knowledges or return fewer fields after a limit failure.
  - A failure provides no partial jq result; the program cannot read host files or environment variables.

  ## Match names or literal text

  1. Obtain the query, intended Home, eligible scope, filters and external source selection.
  2. Confirm whether the task asks for names or literal text; route intent to the “Choose retrieval deliberately” section and dependency effects to the “Trace change impact” section.
  3. Execute the name or text query using the command examples, quoted globs and case rules in Select the question first.

  4. Return matching canonical identities and source evidence, or an explicit empty result with the effective filters and sources.

  Completion: the result answers the search question in the intended Home without inventing matches or silently widening scope.

  ## Choose retrieval deliberately

  1. Obtain the intent, selected Home, eligible sections and filters.
  2. Choose lexical, semantic or hybrid mode before ranking; lexical needs no embedding model.
  - --case-sensitive controls selectors and lexical matching; it does not turn vector similarity into a literal case filter.

  ```sh
  aterm corpus discover 'reopen completed work' --mode lexical --search-in definition --detail
  ```

  3. For semantic or hybrid retrieval, inspect index readiness for the selected Home, corpus and model before relying on results.

  ```sh
  aterm corpus index status
  ```

  4. If the required model/index is unavailable, read _Runtime_Management_Skill_ (“Select the environment before changing it” and “Prepare and verify the selected search mode”). Prepare the selected Home, then retry with the same intent, mode and source selection; report preparation failure if it remains unavailable.

  > Semantic preparation failed, so silently return lexical results — BAD.
  >
  > Report the preparation failure or prepare and retry the requested mode — GOOD.

  5. Inspect candidate Definitions or source evidence and read full owners before relying on their contracts.

  > score = probability that the concept is correct — BAD.
  >
  > score ranks leads within the selected retrieval scope — GOOD.

  Return ranked leads, selection and supporting evidence. Scores are neither confidence nor established Relations.

  ## Read the complete owner

  1. Obtain target selectors and reading purpose; resolve ambiguity rather than guessing a Knowledge.
  2. Read the complete Term Declaration and required context for the canonical identity. term view follows whole-Term daggers transitively, once per identity; a bounded GraphQL excerpt does not establish that this context was read.

  ```sh
  aterm term view _vending_machine:Slot_ --detail
  ```

  3. Use GraphQL for a bounded excerpt or traversal when appropriate; inspect errors and pagination using the “Project only the necessary fields” section.
  4. Read current Scope explicitly for authoring or implementation; distinguish obligations, explanations and unresolved questions.

  > Treat a matching Definition or selected section as the entire contract — BAD.
  >
  > Read the complete owner and required context before asserting its obligations — GOOD.

  Return exact qualified Terms and Term Kinds read, applicable boundaries and unresolved required context. An excerpt or summary never establishes that all obligations were read.

  ## Trace change impact

  ```sh
  aterm term occurrences _inventory:Stock_Item_
  aterm graph relations _inventory:Stock_Item_
  ```

  1. Record the proposed change, baseline identities and effective corpus/external source selection.
  2. Configured externalSources apply by default to rename; explicit source selection replaces that configuration.
  3. Read complete owners and related Terms; inspect mixed responsibilities, duplication, contradictions and missing obligations.
  4. Trace declarations, references, callers and selected external occurrences using both baseline and proposed identities, including moved or removed targets.

  > Count matching spellings as affected identities — BAD.
  >
  > Resolve qualified identity and preserve a same-spelled Term in another Knowledge — GOOD.

  5. Follow incoming derived_from Relations; include affected rewritten guidance in the same review.
  6. Separate direct evidence from inferred effects; record each inference path and its assumptions.
  7. Distinguish intended semantic changes from meaning that must remain stable.
  8. Assign each affected use a preservation or migration decision and define verification evidence.

  A source you did not inspect remains explicitly uninspected. Report evidence paths for inferred effects.
  Return the affected-use inventory, inspected selection, compatibility decisions and verification plan.
  Impact analysis alone does not authorize or apply changes.

.reminder
  - Match query breadth to the question; a focused result does not prove exhaustive coverage.
  - Choose names, literal text, intent or dependency effects deliberately.
  - Record Home, filters and sources; an empty filtered result is not universal absence.
  - Read full owners and writer Scope before relying on obligations.
  - Inspect GraphQL errors and pagination; never mix corpus versions.
  - Use where/orderBy for typed collection filters and sorting; use root jq for aggregation. Preserve jq case rules and follow pagination.
  - Pass changing jq values with field-local bindings; restart pagination after parameter changes.
  - Verify the requested retrieval mode and index; scores rank leads, not confidence.
  - For impact, trace old/new identities and incoming derivations; separate observed, inferred and uninspected effects.
  - Return affected-use decisions and verification evidence; analysis alone applies nothing.
}

skill _Corpus_Authoring_Skill_ = {
  A Skill for expressing supported knowledge as valid TRM and applying verified corpus changes.
.relations
  derived_from _aterm:Viewpoint_
  derived_from _aterm:Method_Selection_Principle_
  requires _Aterm_Basics_Skill_
  derived_from _aterm:Corpus_Notation_Guide_
  derived_from _aterm:Corpus_Notation_Reference_
  derived_from _aterm:Knowledge_Template_
  derived_from _aterm:Term_Declaration_Procedure_
  derived_from _aterm:Model_Term_Declaration_Completion_Procedure_
  derived_from _aterm:TRM_Syntax_Reference_
  derived_from _aterm:Viewpoint_Usage_Guide_
  derived_from _aterm:Edit_Protocol_
  derived_from _aterm:Corpus_Change_Procedure_
  derived_from _aterm:Obligation_Statement_Rule_
  derived_from _aterm:Corpus_Validation_Procedure_
  derived_from _aterm:Structural_Validation_Guidance_
  derived_from _aterm:Corpus_Change_Application_Procedure_
  derived_from _aterm:Source_Authoring_
  derived_from _aterm:Workspace_File_Access_
  derived_from _aterm:Command_Invocation_Guide_
  derived_from _aterm:Syntax_
  derived_from _aterm:Edit_Term_Declarations_
  derived_from _aterm:Rename_Corpus_Identities_
  derived_from _aterm:Move_Terms_
  derived_from _aterm:Knowledge_
  derived_from _aterm:Task_
  derived_from _aterm:Change_Set_
  derived_from _aterm:Draft_
  references _Specification_Implementation_Skill_
  references _Modeling_Skill_
.description
  Use for TRM syntax, Term Declarations, structural validation and guarded corpus writes; decide meaning before writing.
.body
  ## Choose the applicable authoring work

  - For an accepted Term Declaration, wording or Group change, use the syntax and guarded-write sections; do not manufacture a Modeling Report.
  - Use Fill the model from supported answers only when converting Modeling results into Term Declarations.
  - If subject meaning or ownership is unsettled, read _Modeling_Skill_ (“Choose the entry condition” through “Return a consistent final state”). Return with settled identities, supported answers and closure evidence before final authoring.
  - If the task includes any code update, read _Specification_Implementation_Skill_ (“Resolve the intended behavior” and “Implement and verify both sides”) before coding. It owns code/specification synchronization, including behavior-preserving fixes; use this Skill for its corpus writes.

  ## Apply the selected vocabulary

  - Read the selected Viewpoint's complete Markdown before drafting; its explanation and authoring criteria take precedence over this Skill's general guidance.
  - Use its actual Term Kinds and section purposes. Examples below demonstrate source operations, not a universal content schema.
  - Follow Viewpoint-specific authoring and review instructions where applicable; retain required repository review for Aterm-provided Skill changes.

  > Author a Skill through the common write commands after reading `aterm viewpoint view skill`; its description/body/reminder belong to that vocabulary.

  ## Write source, not a rendered view

  - Keep one .trm file per Knowledge: @knowledge first, then @viewpoints, then optional Description and Scope before Term Declarations.
  - Filenames do not define identity or vocabulary; use bound Viewpoint names and a unique lowercase_snake_case Knowledge ID.
  - Description tells readers what is inside; Scope tells writers what belongs, what must be covered and what belongs elsewhere.

  ```trm
  @knowledge inventory
  @viewpoints specification
  @description {
    Stock positions and Product kinds.
  }
  @scope {
    Define stock identity, quantity and Product association; omit screen layout.
  }
  concept _Slot_ in stock = {
    A stock position for one _Product_ kind.
  .relations
    contains _Product_
  .contract
    - Stock count must not be negative.
  }
  concept _Product_ = {
    A kind of item identified by name.
  }
  ```

  - Header blocks need nonempty two-space-indented bodies and column-zero closing braces.
  - A Term Declaration declares a local Term name, optional `in group.path` and its Term Kind at column zero.
  - The unheaded body is Definition. Put later sections in Schema order; indent their content two spaces.
  - Rendered // Knowledge, // Viewpoint and // Scope annotations are not source directives.

  > Copy a multi-Knowledge read view into one source file — BAD.
  >
  > Write each Knowledge separately and qualify cross-Knowledge references — GOOD.

  ## Preserve identity and organization

  - The unique identity of both Term and Term Declaration is Knowledge ID + local Term name.
  - Each Term has exactly one Term Declaration with one Term Kind and optional Group. A second declaration is always an error; remove the retired allowMultipleEntriesPerTerm setting.
  - A short and qualified spelling of the same Term Kind do not create distinct Term Declarations.
  - Only use a short Term Kind when one declared Viewpoint owns it; qualification does not import a Viewpoint.
  - Classification text does not choose a declaration Term Kind.

  > Generic classification says procedure, so declare Term Kind procedure — BAD.
  >
  > Declare generic's `term` Term Kind and put procedure in classification — GOOD.

  - Use dot-separated lowercase_snake_case Group paths for authored organization, not structural Relations invented for display.

  ```sh
  aterm term list --knowledge inventory --tree
  ```

  - The tree is File / Group / Term Declaration; derived part Groups and temporary Hidden Groups are not authored Groups.
  - Change a Group through a complete-Term Declaration term edit; term move transfers the Term Declaration to a compatible Knowledge.

  ```diff
  *** Begin Patch
  *** Update Term: _inventory:Stock_Item_
  @@
  -entity _Stock_Item_ in stock = {
  +entity _Stock_Item_ in stock.inventory = {
     One tracked stock identity.
   }
  *** End Patch
  ```

  - This patch assumes the complete Term Declaration shown by the context; use the target's actual Term Kind and content. Omitting a section suffix selects the complete Term Declaration.

  ## Declare connections and expand only the intended content

  - Every non-self reference needs a target declaration in this Term Declaration's .relations immediately after Definition.
  - A declaration is one built-in or one-to-five-word phrase and a whole-Term target, without a bullet or source token.
  - Use references when evidence supports no stronger assertion; built-in underscore names use exact lowercase spelling.
  - is_a expresses Generalization, has_part Parthood, has_member Membership and has_state permitted State membership.
  - These Relations do not copy obligations, select a current/initial State, infer transitions or imply lifecycle ownership.
  - Put cardinality, initial/transition and no-partial-effect requirements in their owning content sections.
  - References name a whole Term; a section selector must match a present section of its Term Declaration.
  - A plain section reference does not expand. Put a section dagger alone where its content applies.

  ```trm
  .relations
    applies _Shared_Rule_
  .contract
    _Shared_Rule_.contract†
  ```

  > Put the section dagger inside a sentence — BAD.
  >
  > Put it alone immediately below the applicable instruction — GOOD.

  - Expansion preserves Knowledge context and adds no heading; missing sections, duplicate Terms and cycles fail checks.
  - Whole-Term daggers append complete Term Declarations in term view; skill prerequisite reading is instead declared with requires.
  - Fenced and indented code are opaque; other recognized Term tokens must resolve. Escape hypothetical names and write retired names as plain words.

  ## Fill the model from supported answers

  Apply this section to supported modeling answers and explicit uncertainty. Read _Modeling_Skill_ for unfamiliar distinctions. Save partial knowledge only when authorized and permitted by Scope and Schema; structural validity alone does not settle meaning.

  1. Use retained Terms, selected schemas, retelling and recorded distinctions; write each Definition in one or two sentences.
  2. Declare retelling Relations on their active side and declare remaining non-self references.
  3. Validate the candidate using guarded dry-run or an isolated corpus when needed. Settle questions before writing dependent Rules; independent supported content may proceed with explicit uncertainty.
  4. Put each supported answer and permitted pending question in an appropriate section; a Viewpoint without a Rule section may finish with descriptive content.

  - For supported Generalization, declare is_a and state the difference. When filling supported Rule sections, expand the general Term's relevant Rule section at its point of application using the section dagger taught above; the Relation itself copies nothing.

  5. Write actual obligations with a subject, condition and required behavior; keep descriptions descriptive.
  6. Put Relation rules on the owning Term Declaration and refusal/no-partial-effect rules on the refusing behavior.
  7. Record Chosen and Non-goal decisions in a permitted explanatory section; place any resulting obligation on its actual owner too.
  8. Keep subject wording consistent. Compare Rules and Differs with the filled Term Declarations and resolve omissions.
  9. Reopen modeling if new answers expose identity or coverage gaps; do not conceal them in Remarks.
  10. Deliver complete Knowledges, check the final corpus and review Scope coverage again.

  Completion: each answer has a permitted destination, reference closure holds, and the accepted content is represented without disguising pending decisions as commitments.

  ## Preserve identities across commands

  - Qualify Knowledge when a Term name is ambiguous; an optional Term Kind prefix guards the selected Term Declaration's classification; use a full Term Declaration edit for a Group change.

  ```diff
  *** Begin Patch
  *** Update Term: skill.procedure _inventory:Stock_Check_.procedure
  @@
  -  Read stock.
  +  Read stock from the selected source.
  *** End Patch
  ```

  - External rename replaces exact qualified Term tokens in comments, strings and code; it does not infer code identifiers or replace unqualified literal tokens.
  - Preview the combined source diff before applying an external rename; selected files must be UTF-8 text and symlinks are not followed.
  - term move transfers each Term's single Term Declaration to an existing Knowledge with a compatible Viewpoint-qualified Term Kind; it preserves sections and Groups and rewrites incoming, outgoing and configured external references.
  - A move adds a Term Kind qualifier when the destination makes the old short Term Kind ambiguous. term format only orders Relation declarations.

  ## Check the actual state

  ```sh
  aterm corpus check
  ```

  1. Confirm the intended executable, Home and saved content before running the check.
  2. After writes, confirm the saved bytes independently with file read or local filesystem reading. For server-backed queries, wait for debounce and compare term show (or knowledge list for a new file) with the saved change before running corpus check.
  3. Recheck if a write occurred during verification. If the published state never catches up, report it as unverified; inspecting bytes alone is not a structural check of those bytes.

  > Check before saving and report the old success after a write — BAD.
  >
  > Check the final saved state and report its actual diagnostics — GOOD.

  4. Run corpus check and report diagnostics, validation status and the state verified.

  A successful invocation may still report diagnostics. A caller requiring a valid corpus must resolve them and check again.
  A clean check establishes syntax, schema and reference validity. It does not prove correct ownership, complete requirements or a useful model.

  ## Prepare and choose the write

  - Keep the intended change, original baseline, complete proposed diff, affected files and effective source selection.
  - Carry that baseline and verification obligations through corrections; do not replace them with the latest partial result.
  - Read complete Knowledge source before replacing it and preserve unrelated Term Declarations.
  - Add, update or delete Term Declarations: term edit with Add Term, Update Term or Delete Term blocks. Identity spelling: term rename.
  - Whole-Term transfer: term move; the complete Term Declaration moves with its Term. A Group change uses a complete Term Declaration edit.
  - New Knowledge: knowledge create with a source-relative .trm path and complete source.
  - New Term Declaration in an existing Knowledge: Add Term with a qualified identity or --knowledge and its complete declaration.
  - Knowledge header: knowledge edit with Update Knowledge hunks. Send complete source only for deliberate whole-Knowledge replacement.
  - Viewpoint document and binding: viewpoint create/edit/delete.
  - Knowledge identity: knowledge rename. Formatting only: term format. Choose operation granularity from the intended change.
  - Read the selected Term Kind schema; prepare complete declarations and all non-self references.
  - Default package Knowledge and Viewpoints are read-only unless allowDefaultWrites is true; keep project content in a project Knowledge.

  ```sh
  aterm term edit --file changes.patch --dry-run
  aterm term edit --file changes.patch
  aterm knowledge create inventory --path docs/inventory.trm --file inventory.trm --dry-run
  ```

  For a header change, run aterm knowledge edit inventory --file headers.patch --dry-run, then repeat without --dry-run. Through MCP, use cmd: "knowledge edit inventory --dry-run" and supply the patch in stdin; omit --file. Read the current header first; optionally use --if-match with the content version from file read.

  ```diff
  *** Begin Patch
  *** Update Knowledge: inventory
  @@
   @description {
  -  Inventory concepts.
  +  Stock identities, quantities and storage positions.
   }
  *** End Patch
  ```

  - Header hunks can edit @description, @scope and @viewpoints; they cannot reach Term bodies or insert declarations.
  - Changing @viewpoints still validates every existing Term. Use knowledge rename to change @knowledge.

  ```diff
  *** Begin Patch
  *** Update Term: _inventory:Stock_Item_.definition
  @@
  -  One tracked stock identity.
  +  One tracked stock identity that survives a label change.
  *** End Patch
  ```

  Add and Delete use the same patch envelope. This example assumes Unused_Storage_Bin exists and has no remaining references:

  ```diff
  *** Begin Patch
  *** Add Term: _inventory:Storage_Bin_
  +concept _Storage_Bin_ = {
  +  A named storage position for stock.
  +}
  *** Delete Term: _inventory:Unused_Storage_Bin_
  *** End Patch
  ```

  - Add supplies one complete declaration with + on every line; the local declaration name must match the selector.
  - Add appends to an existing Knowledge; create the Knowledge first when its identity is new.
  - Delete supplies only the selector; repair remaining references or delete their owners in the same patch.
  - Combine mutually referring additions and dependent updates in one patch; references are checked on the final candidate.

  > Send the complete large Knowledge to add two Terms — unnecessary replacement.
  >
  > Send two Add Term blocks and the needed Update Term blocks — preserves unrelated declarations.

  The patch is appropriate only when that replacement preserves the agreed meaning.
  Use a complete Term Declaration edit to add sections or change its Group; section patches cannot change delimiters.

  ## Apply and verify

  1. Run the check in Check the actual state on the baseline; resolve diagnostics or report blockers before dependent writes. After a repair, repeat validation on the repaired state.
  2. Preview the selected operation with --dry-run and inspect the complete proposed diff.
  3. Verify the baseline is still current; use a content version guard where the command supports it.
  4. Apply the reviewed operation and inspect saved effects, including partial failures.
  5. Run the check in Check the actual state on the saved state.
  6. Compare intended and saved changes; correct through preview, save and validation again.
  7. Review every derivedImpacts target; update affected guidance or record why it still applies.
  8. Finish only when the intended final state is saved and checked without unresolved structural diagnostics; otherwise return remaining diagnostics and blockers.

  ## Recovery

  - Use file list/read to inspect malformed source or configuration; file read returns text and a content version.
  - file write/delete --if-match VERSION refuses stale input. Inspect saved status and diagnostics separately.
  - A migration may need raw writes across temporarily invalid files; repair all affected files and check before semantic authoring resumes.
  - knowledge delete refuses references; viewpoint delete refuses Knowledges still using it.
  - Git metadata and .git pointers are reserved for local Git tooling, including during dry-run.
  - Compare the saved corpus diff with the requested effect; the code/specification checks selected before coding still apply to behavior-preserving fixes.
  - A Group-only change preserves Term Declaration identities and Relations; compare the selected tree before and after.
  - Return the saved changes, reviewed diff and final verification evidence; do not describe a partial save as success.
.reminder
  - Use the actual Knowledge boundary and Term Kind schema; declare every non-self reference per Term Declaration.
  - Resolve questions before writing dependent Rules; preserve supported content and permitted explicit uncertainty.
  - Preserve the original baseline, source selection, complete diff and unrelated content.
  - Validate, preview, inspect, save, then validate the final saved snapshot.
  - Review every derivedImpacts target; update it or explain why it still applies.
  - Inspect partial saves and final effects; structural validity does not prove semantic coverage.
}

