  derived_from _aterm:Consistent_Naming_Principle_
  references _Naming_Skill_
  references _Corpus_Reading_Skill_
  references _Corpus_Authoring_Skill_
  references _Viewpoint_Authoring_Skill_
.description
  Use when subjects, meanings, boundaries or obligations are unsettled; develop supported meanings and identify what remains unresolved before dependent Rules or actions.
.body
  ## Choose the entry condition

  - For a new model, start with the source account and candidate ideas; for a revision, preserve the original model and affected uses.
  - Use the sections below as modeling tools. Questions, examples, counterexamples, diagrams and experiments can be combined in a useful order.
  - A model-only task returns supported identities, answers, boundaries and uncertainty without saving.
  - Before saving, read _Corpus_Authoring_Skill_ for notation, guarded writes and validation.
  - If the vocabulary misses a recurring distinction, report it and use _Viewpoint_Authoring_Skill_ when a vocabulary change is in scope; a guidance gap alone does not block useful modeling.

  ## Develop the supported account

  - Obtain the requested outcome, source account, selected Viewpoint and current owners with Scope.
  - Use _Model_Review_Skill_ to distinguish source facts, observations, Chosen decisions and unknowns.
  - Preserve access to source evidence; a concise account is sufficient when it retains the relevant meaning.
  - Choose the question or example most likely to clarify the current boundary; do not replay known answers just to follow a sequence.

  > Task identity is clear, but reopening is unspecified — retain Task and keep reopening Open.
  >
  > Copy familiar product behavior into the account as a fact — BAD.

  - Give an obligation one owner whose Definition supports it; an existing Term does not prove ownership.
  - Check the complete resulting responsibilities when ownership changes.

  > Task Manager hides responsibility — BAD.
  >
  > Task owns completion State; Complete Task owns repeated-completion behavior; Task List owns membership — clearer boundaries.

  ## Seed candidates from the selected vocabulary

  - Read the complete selected Viewpoint Markdown: admission, Term Kinds, section purposes, questions, retention and its authoring explanation.
  - Its guidance takes precedence over this Skill's general guidance; adapt the method to the vocabulary instead of importing another Viewpoint's Rules or sections.
  - Identify useful subjects and acts from evidence; the first candidate list need not be complete.
  - Search existing meanings before committing a new identity and use _Naming_Skill_.
  - For the first vocabulary, use its Establish the naming context section: identify core concepts, then test supporting names together before treating them as examples to copy.
  - Provisional labels are useful during exploration; settle canonical identity before applying a rename or declaration.

  ```sh
  aterm viewpoint view domain
  aterm term list '*Task*'
  ```

  - Examples demonstrate a vocabulary; they are not facts about the current subject.
  - Code identifiers are evidence, not authority to copy incidental names.

  ## Use questions to check coverage

  - Use section questions, difference questions and missed-content questions as coverage aids.
  - Reorder or combine questions and reuse supported answers. Investigate unanswered questions that matter to the intended claim.
  - A frame helps express an answer and locate its section; it does not prescribe the order of thought.
  - Add a useful practitioner question when the grid misses something; report recurring gaps without first requiring a new Skill or Viewpoint.
  - Compare confusable candidates and ask what the chosen method has not exposed.

  > A State Transition diagram reveals the missing cancellation boundary — use it, then check relevant questions.
  >
  > A completed review returns findings, therefore the review refused to run — BAD; findings are a result, refusal means it did not run.

  ## Interpret meaning, not surface grammar

  - A Definition’s head noun identifies a category, not automatically a new Term or declaration Term Kind.
  - Consider whole answers, actual behavior and uses before splitting, merging or introducing a State.

  > A and B can suggest combined responsibilities; an interval’s start and end can still define one subject.
  >
  > A that B can suggest a borrowed property; an active subscriber can also be a meaningful role.
  >
  > A or B can suggest States; card or cash can instead be payment methods.

  - A substantive subject may earn a candidate; excluded detail stays on its supported owner.
  - Put actual obligations in Rules with subject, condition and required behavior; declare supported connections as Relations.
  - `nothing` means the source does not answer; keep it Open. `none` means the source explicitly establishes absence.
  - Compare duplicate meanings and distinctions; renaming requires supported identity, not a prettier phrase.
  - Reconcile affected answers and references before relying on or saving the result. Exploratory notes may keep identifiable alternatives.

  ## Prune from substantive answers

  - Apply the Viewpoint’s retention criteria to independent meaning, responsibilities and uses.
  - Keep a useful subject even when discovering it exposes more questions than it answers.

  > Reservation reveals expiry, cancellation and ownership questions — investigate its value; do not reject it by unknown count.

  - Drop redundant or unsupported candidates with a reason. Move still-relevant obligations and unknowns to their supported owner rather than erasing them.
  - A category, synonym or rationale alone does not establish independent content.

  > An Explanation is not executable, so drop it — BAD; descriptive knowledge can have independent value.

  ## Inspect whole passes, then repeat

  - Inspect duplicated responsibilities, missing Relations and repeated unexplained conditions as the model evolves.
  - Repeated actors, places or conditions are clues, not automatic Terms or States.
  - Revisit questions and affected uses after material identity or boundary changes; avoid repeating unaffected work.
  - Stability can support confidence, but stable names alone do not settle missing requirements.

  ## Retell and find omissions

  - Retell the whole model within the task's stated Scope using its canonical Terms and explicit connections; compare it with the original account, not only the latest edits.
  - Use scenarios, tables, diagrams or experiments to discover gaps; preserve a connected Term-based retelling as the inspectable result.

  > Plugin Renderer renders a Document; Document Action reaches Plugin Action Handler, which may change the Document and send a User Message.
  >
  > The account requires CLI and MCP, but the retelling reaches only the CLI — investigate the missing MCP path.

  - For an initial model, plan two or three retelling passes; the first draft is a starting point, not completion evidence.
  - After resolving material gaps, retell the corrected whole again; reviewing only changed Terms can miss their new interactions.
  - Continue when another pass exposes material gaps; a pass count, stable names, Open: 0 or a clean corpus check does not prove coverage.
  - Finish the supported scope when the latest retelling and source comparison reveal no unresolved material gap in that claim; expose blocked questions and supported partial results when evidence runs out.
  - For a revision, bound the retelling to the affected subject and its relevant connections; a wording-only edit does not require rebuilding the model.
  - Show important subjects and their connections; use precise Relation phrases for supported assertions.
  - Map relevant source meaning to modeled content, explicit pending questions or justified boundaries.
  - An unexplained noun may reveal a Term, a field, a Rule or merely ambiguous wording; investigate instead of automatically adding it.
  - Ask what the whole account is about and what a practitioner might notice beyond the grid.
  - Label external ideas and adopted design choices rather than presenting them as source facts.

  ## Maintain the Report as the model changes

  - Keep enough evidence to explain supported meaning, important decisions, identity changes and material uncertainty.
  - A complex revision may benefit from Terms, Relations, Rules, Given, Chosen, Non-goal, Differs, Renamed, Split, Merged, Dropped, Asked and Open records.
  - A small change can use a concise comparison; the Report is an aid, not a required transcript of thought.
  - Given records supported facts; Chosen records adopted decisions; Non-goal records a justified boundary; Open records missing knowledge and what it blocks.

  > Open: retry behavior unknown; blocks the retry Rule and implementation — useful.
  >
  > Open: 12, all closable — not evidence that anything is settled.

  ## Return a consistent final state

  - State what the result claims to cover and what remains unresolved.
  - End a modeling report by explaining the resulting connected model to the user with its canonical Terms, including material conditions, outcomes and unresolved boundaries.
  - A Term inventory, change list or link to a report cannot replace this explanation; ordinary connective prose is welcome.

  > Added Document Action and Plugin Action Handler — a change list.
  >
  > A Document Action carries the rendered Document Version to the Plugin Action Handler; a changed version refuses the write — a model explanation.

  - If explaining the result exposes another gap, resolve it and retell the affected whole again, or identify the Open boundary instead of claiming completion.
  - Settle a question before writing a Rule or performing an operation whose validity depends on its answer.
  - Supported independent facts and Rules can be saved with explicit uncertainty when authorized and permitted by Scope and Schema.
  - Preserve structural validation and established behavioral contracts; do not hide required missing behavior in Remarks.

  > Confirmed lodging with pending transport — useful partial knowledge.
  >
  > Invent a departure time to make Open: none — BAD.

  - Do not claim whole-model completeness while in-scope questions remain. Ask about consequential blocked choices and continue authorized independent work.
  - A model-only revision returns its baseline, supported changes and affected-use preservation/migration plan to the applying caller.

  ## Save a new model

  - Draft supported Term Declarations using the selected Knowledge boundaries and Schemas; declare every non-self reference.
  - Use _Model_Review_Skill_ to check ownership and source coverage. Existing current evidence can satisfy the review without replaying the method.
  - Resolve findings that undermine the saved claims; keep permitted pending questions explicit.
  - Validate the candidate with guarded dry-run or an isolated corpus when the change needs it.
  - Use _Corpus_Authoring_Skill_ to apply the accepted change and verify the saved result.
  - Return what was saved, its supported boundary, remaining questions and verification limits.

  ## Revise an existing model

  - Preserve the original baseline; examine affected responsibilities and dependencies.

  > Add account permissions to Task List because its file exists — BAD.
  >
  > Compare its Definition with the new responsibility and settle the owner — GOOD.

  - Plan splits, merges, renames or moves from supported identities and boundaries.
  - Use _Corpus_Reading_Skill_ to trace affected uses and _Model_Review_Skill_ to compare meaning and coverage.
  - Include preservation and migration changes in the accepted draft; a wording-only correction does not require a migration exercise.
  - Apply through _Corpus_Authoring_Skill_; verify affected saved uses, reference closure and the stated semantic boundary.
  - Report blockers and partial results precisely; review and structural validity alone do not establish complete knowledge.
.reminder
  - Preserve source evidence and baseline; distinguish facts, Chosen decisions and Open questions.
  - Choose useful questions, examples or experiments; grammar and unknown counts are clues, not decisions.
  - Retain independent meaning; reconcile identities and check relevant source coverage.
  - Resolve unknowns before dependent Rules or actions; permitted partial knowledge can remain explicit.
  - Retell with canonical Terms, compare the original account, resolve gaps and retell the corrected whole; expect two or three passes for a first model.
  - End the user report with the connected Term-based explanation and Open boundaries; pass counts and clean checks do not prove completeness.
  - A model-only revision returns its impact plan; authorized writes use Corpus Authoring Skill and verify affected saved uses.
}

