  derived_from _aterm:Specification_Synchronization_Rule_
  derived_from _aterm:Consistent_Naming_Principle_
  derived_from _aterm:Semantic_Review_Guidance_
  derived_from _aterm:Corpus_Validation_Procedure_
  derived_from _aterm:Specification_Synchronization_Rule_
  references _Modeling_Skill_
.description
  Use to add or revise Rules, implement specified behavior or repair code while preserving justified ownership and Scope coverage.
.body
  ## Choose the intended effect

  - A Rule-only change uses the ownership and Rule-writing section below.
  - An implementation task also uses Resolve the intended behavior and Implement and verify both sides.
  - Do not infer a code change from a request to discuss or revise a specification alone.
  - Read the selected Viewpoint Markdown; its guidance takes precedence over general Skill guidance. This Skill does not require the specification Viewpoint or a contract section; use the actual vocabulary for supported obligations.
  - Choose a reasoning order that clarifies behavior and ownership. Reuse current review evidence across the sections below; a route label does not require a second identical review.
  - Resolve missing decisions before dependent implementation; supported independent work may continue with explicit pending questions.

  ## Establish ownership and preserve closure

  1. Perform _Model_Review_Skill_ (“Check the resulting responsibility”).
  2. If a subject is missing, an identity conflicts or ownership is unresolved, use _Modeling_Skill_ (“Choose the entry condition” through “Return a consistent final state”) for a new or revised model without saving. Return with settled owners for the proposed obligations, explicit remaining questions and any revision impact plan; include the accepted model and preservation/migration changes in this task's final change set.
  3. Write supported obligations on their owners with one obligation per bullet, using _Corpus_Authoring_Skill_.

  > The completion filter requires only active Tasks — incomplete if matches may be omitted.
  >
  > The filter returns every active Task and no completed Task; Task owns completion state — precise ownership and coverage.

  4. Perform _Model_Review_Skill_ (“Review the complete subject”) and resolve findings before accepting the draft.
  5. Repeat affected ownership, semantic and application checks after corrections; derive the change set from the accepted draft and original baseline.
  6. Return blockers instead of applying an unaccepted state; otherwise perform _Corpus_Authoring_Skill_ (“Apply and verify”).

  After application, verify affected saved uses against any revision impact plan. Return updated Rules and ownership/coverage evidence. Completion requires that obligations are neither duplicated across owners nor unsupported by their Definitions.

  ## Resolve the intended behavior

  1. Read complete owners and Knowledge Scope before coding; obtain the account, current implementation and proposed change.
  2. Perform _Model_Review_Skill_ (“Check the resulting responsibility”) and compare code, specification and intended behavior.
  3. Choose whether code or specification must change from user intent and evidence; existing code alone cannot justify weakening a contract.

  > A filter drops a matching Task; weaken the every-match requirement — BAD.
  >
  > Preserve the requirement, fix the filter and test the omitted-match case — GOOD.

  - Treat missing in-scope requirements as specification gaps. Add supported requirements and record new decisions as Chosen.
  - Do not infer Scope from filenames, silently widen it or turn incidental implementation details into obligations.
  - Expose consequential unresolved choices; if identity or ownership is unsettled, use the model-only route in _Modeling_Skill_ and resume dependent implementation when its identities and required decisions are supported.

  ## Implement and verify both sides

  4. When required behavior changes, follow the “Establish ownership and preserve closure” section.
  5. Implement the agreed behavior and synchronize its owning specification in the same task.
  6. For unchanged intended behavior, preserve the contract and record correction rationale and verification in its explanatory section.

  > Invent a behavioral requirement just to obtain a SPEC diff — BAD.
  >
  > Record the behavior-preserving fix and the scenario that verifies it — GOOD.

  7. Describe changed inputs, defaults, conditions, refusals, result information and state changes; form any additional specification change set.
  8. Review additional specification changes with _Model_Review_Skill_ (“Check the resulting responsibility” and “Review the complete subject”). Resolve findings and repeat affected checks before accepting the draft; only then perform _Corpus_Authoring_Skill_ (“Apply and verify”).
  9. Perform _Model_Review_Skill_ (“Review the complete subject”) and _Corpus_Authoring_Skill_ (“Check the actual state”); run implementation tests that can contradict the claimed behavior.
  10. Compare code/specification diffs and Scope coverage. Resolve findings that undermine behavior, ownership or consistency; rebuild and refresh affected checks after correction.
  11. If required evidence is unavailable or blocking findings remain, report incomplete with affected Term Declarations and unresolved gaps. Otherwise report the verified behavior, synchronized Term Declarations and evidence.

  Completion: evidence supports the implemented behavior and its specification has justified ownership. A clean corpus check alone does not establish semantic or implementation correctness.
.reminder
  - Justify one owner per obligation; unresolved meaning returns to Modeling Skill.
  - Compare intent, complete owners, Scope and code; code alone cannot weaken a contract.
  - Write one supported obligation per bullet; resolve semantic findings before saving.
  - Synchronize implementation and specification in the same task when code changes.
  - Explain behavior-preserving fixes without inventing requirements.
  - Test scenarios that could contradict the claim; compare both diffs and final coverage.
}


skill _Runtime_Management_Skill_ = {
  A Skill for configuring Aterm, preparing its services and search, and managing installed Skill connections.
.relations
  derived_from _aterm:Method_Selection_Principle_
  derived_from _aterm:Usage_Log_
  requires _Aterm_Basics_Skill_
  derived_from _aterm:Command_Invocation_Guide_
  derived_from _aterm:Configuration_
  derived_from _aterm:HTTP_Server_
  derived_from _aterm:MCP_Server_
  derived_from _aterm:Skill_Installation_Management_Procedure_
  derived_from _aterm:Guidance_Catalog_
  derived_from _aterm:Task_
  derived_from _aterm:Environment_
  derived_from _aterm:Aterm_Basics_Guide_
  derived_from _aterm:Runtime_Configuration_Guidance_
  derived_from _aterm:Command_Invocation_Guide_
  derived_from _aterm:Configuration_
  derived_from _aterm:HTTP_Server_
  derived_from _aterm:MCP_Server_
  derived_from _aterm:Skill_Installation_Management_Procedure_
  derived_from _aterm:Guidance_Catalog_
  derived_from _aterm:Runtime_Configuration_Guidance_
  derived_from _aterm:Server_Configuration_Procedure_
  derived_from _aterm:Configuration_
  derived_from _aterm:Task_
  derived_from _aterm:Environment_
  derived_from _aterm:Change_Set_
  derived_from _aterm:Aterm_Basics_Guide_
  derived_from _aterm:Skill_Installation_Guidance_
  derived_from _aterm:Command_Invocation_Guide_
  derived_from _aterm:Configuration_
  derived_from _aterm:HTTP_Server_
  derived_from _aterm:MCP_Server_
  derived_from _aterm:Skill_Installation_Management_Procedure_
  derived_from _aterm:Guidance_Catalog_
  derived_from _aterm:Skill_Installation_Guidance_
  derived_from _aterm:Skill_Installation_Management_Procedure_
  derived_from _aterm:Change_Set_
.description
  Use for Home and source configuration, server or MCP lifecycle, search preparation, and managed Skill install, update or uninstall.
.body
  ## Choose the runtime operation

  - Select the intended environment before configuration, service, index or installation work.
  - Run only the applicable operation; configuring a Home does not require restarting a server or reinstalling Skills.
  - If the requested mode is unavailable, explain the limitation and propose a supported alternative. Use it only when it satisfies the authorized outcome; do not silently change the target environment or bypass lifecycle constraints.

  ## Select the environment before changing it

  - Obtain the requested outcome, intended executable, Home and current effective settings.
  - For a persistent edit, save the original baseline and proposed diff; preparation-only work does not require an invented source change.
  - Configuration lives in the selected .aterm/aterm.yaml. Sources and externalSources resolve from its Workspace; Viewpoint bindings resolve from Home.

  ```yaml
  sources: [docs]
  useDefaultKnowledge: true
  useViewpoints: [specification, generic, domain, skill]
  allowDefaultWrites: false
  externalSources:
    core: packages/core/src/**/*.ts
  server:
    host: 127.0.0.1
    port: 43127
    debounceMs: 200
    watchExternal: true
  ```

  - Confirm path bases before moving configuration between projects.

  > `.aterm/aterm.yaml` + `sources: [docs]` selects Workspace/docs — GOOD.
  >
  > Assume the same source selects Workspace/.aterm/docs — BAD.

  - externalSources participates in search and rename even without a server; named matches appear as ext.core.
  - After init, packaged Skills are available without adding package docs to sources.
  - After changing source, inspect recorded ownership in “Establish what is managed”, then “Choose the lifecycle operation”: install for an absent managed installation, update for an existing one.
  - useDefaultKnowledge: false hides implicit package Knowledge from ordinary corpus queries; Skill reading still use packaged guidance.
  - useViewpoints selects the built-in specification, generic, domain and skill vocabularies; omit it for all four, or use [] for none. Custom viewpoints bindings add or override names.
  - Packaged Knowledge and Viewpoints are read-only unless allowDefaultWrites is true; explicit source paths do not bypass protection.

  > Hide defaults: useDefaultKnowledge: false + useViewpoints: [] — skill view still reads packaged guidance.
  >
  > Author a project Knowledge with project content; opt into default writes only when editing the package itself.
  - Do not silently widen source selection or replace a global installation while configuring one project.

  ## Run the intended service

  ```sh
  aterm --home /work/project/.aterm corpus check
  # Choose one startup mode; these are alternatives.
  aterm --home /work/project/.aterm server run
  aterm --home /work/project/.aterm mcp run
  aterm --home /work/project/.aterm mcp run --with-server
  ```

  - --home applies to one invocation; keep it on each command when working outside the target Workspace.
  - Standalone mcp run is client-managed stdio: normal server discovery for reads, otherwise direct filesystem access.
  - --with-server hosts stdio and HTTP in one process, requires a configured or explicit port, refuses an occupied port and stops HTTP when stdio closes.
  - server run serves remote MCP at /mcp. Remote paths refer to that server's Workspace.
  - Stdio stdout carries only MCP protocol; diagnostics belong on stderr.
  - Server reads use a watched snapshot; debounceMs batches filesystem changes. watchExternal: false disables background external-source watching, not explicit source queries.
  - Automatic local CLI and standalone stdio writes go directly to files; allow debounce or verify the final filesystem state.
  - Hosted stdio, remote HTTP MCP and explicit --server writes publish before completion.
  - host: 127.0.0.1 is local-only; 0.0.0.0 listens on IPv4 interfaces; :: selects IPv6 interfaces.
  - For external controllers, configure a controlToken of at least 32 characters and provide it to the client; use a proxy for HTTPS and authentication.
  - Set publicOrigin to the externally presented origin behind a proxy. Public corpus reads and authenticated controls have different access rules.
  - Inspect the current server identity before restarting it. New installed code and changed host, port or token need a restart.

  > CLI --version changed, therefore an old running server has new code — BAD.
  >
  > Restart the intended server and verify its health and behavior — GOOD.

  - If another Home owns the port, select a free server.port; do not stop that unrelated server.
  - Recovery can start with explicit --home and --port; inspect invalid configuration through file read/write with content-version guards.

  ## Investigate a slow or unanswered request

  - Read usage.jsonl inside the selected Home; logging is automatic for CLI, MCP and HTTP execution.
  - Match requestId across HTTP, MCP tool and command spans; spanId identifies one layer.
  - Compare start, phase and finish records before retrying a write.

  > Last phase input: input has not finished reading. Last phase publish: the write returned and snapshot publication is pending.
  >
  > HTTP disconnected: read the current version and saved content before deciding whether another write is needed.

  ```sh
  tail -n 40 /work/project/.aterm/usage.jsonl
  ```

  - HTTP responses expose X-Aterm-Request-Id; inputBytes and output byte counts identify large payloads without recording their contents.
  - The file stays within 5 MiB by retaining roughly the newest 4 MiB of complete records; old request records can disappear.
  - A missing finish does not prove an unsaved write; a missing start does not prove the request never arrived if logging failed or records were removed.
  - Log failure produces a bounded stderr warning and does not change the operation result. Stdio stdout remains MCP protocol only.

  ## Prepare and verify the selected search mode

  ```sh
  aterm corpus index build
  aterm corpus index status
  ```

  - Native embedding preparation may download a model; later semantic searches reuse local model and index data.
  - Check readiness for the actual Home, sources, model and selected search mode.
  - After preparation, return to the original discovery request with the same intent, mode and source selection.
  - Do not silently replace semantic or hybrid discovery with lexical search when preparation fails.
  - Confirm effective source selection, server behavior or index readiness as applicable; report unsuccessful and unverified effects explicitly.
  - Installation, synchronization and server/MCP lifecycle management are local-only operations. Remote corpus and index commands remain available.

  ## Establish what is managed

  - Read the authoritative Skill sources, managed-name manifest, current generated documents and configured Agent connections.
  - Retain the original baseline, intended changes, proposed diff, affected files, selected sources and verification obligations through installation retries.
  - Skill discovery, reading, MCP and installation share one package-plus-Home catalog, independently of ordinary default-resource visibility.
  - Names derive from complete qualified Terms, lowercased with underscores and the namespace separator changed to hyphens; invalid or colliding names are refused.
  - Run skill update to replace the previous managed set; configured sync/uninstall commands update Agent connections and remove stale managed names.
  - Change authoritative corpus content; installed SKILL.md files are snapshots.

  > Fix a generated SKILL.md and lose the change at update — BAD.
  >
  > Fix the corpus source, inspect its rendered output, then update — GOOD.

  - Each installed SKILL.md contains the exact generated TOC; other Term Kinds are not installed merely because they are referenced.
  - Inventory presence does not prove installation, synchronization or activation in an already running Agent.

  ## Choose the lifecycle operation

  ```sh
  aterm skill list
  aterm skill install
  aterm skill update
  aterm skill uninstall
  ```

  - Use install when no managed installation is recorded; use update to synchronize changed content and removed names from an existing recorded installation.
  - uninstall removes the recorded managed names; do not infer ownership from a matching directory name.
  - Aterm stages generated content under .aterm/skills and tracks ownership and pending synchronization in its manifest.
  - Inspect complete generated Markdown, current and removed names, manifest membership and each synchronization outcome.
  - Preserve user-modified, unmanaged and legacy files when an ownership guard refuses the operation; resolve the conflict before retrying.

  > A directory has the right Skill name, so overwrite it — BAD.
  >
  > Verify its recorded ownership and current content first — GOOD.

  ## Synchronize the intended Agents

  - skills.sync and skills.uninstall must be supplied together or omitted together; they are Bash commands; read their current templates and documented environment before changing them.
  - init supplies project-scoped npx skills connections for Claude Code and Codex; these commands may need network access.
  - Default removal covers managed names across all Agents so stale shared-directory links do not remain.
  - That removal can affect another Agent's connection to the same managed name; inspect the intended name set before running it.
  - The synchronization environment supplies absolute paths and current/removed Skill-name lists; preserve those meanings when customizing commands.
  - Without configured synchronization, generated local documents do not imply Agent connections.
  - On partial failure, preserve the recorded recovery state, inspect completed effects and retry the intended pending operation.
  - An Agent that already read an older Skill does not automatically replace that conversation context after filesystem update.

  Completion: source targets, complete generated documents, manifest membership and applicable Agent synchronization match the request. Report failures and pending or unverified effects explicitly. Run this lifecycle locally; remote MCP does not install Skills on the client machine.
.reminder
  - An unavailable mode calls for an explicit supported alternative, not a silent environment change.
  - Confirm executable, Home and effective settings; preserve the edit baseline.
  - Sources are Workspace-relative; Viewpoint bindings are Home-relative.
  - Verify the intended server and published state; account for debounce and occupied ports.
  - Prepare the requested discovery mode and current index without silently changing modes.
  - Change authoritative Skill sources; select lifecycle operations from recorded ownership.
  - Verify generated TOCs, manifest and Agent synchronization; preserve modified files and pending recovery state.
  - Installation does not replace context already read by a running Agent.
}

skill _Explorer_Presentation_Skill_ = {
  A Skill for presenting Terms, a Working Set and explanatory Notes in an intended browser Session.
.relations
  derived_from _aterm:Method_Selection_Principle_
  derived_from _aterm:GraphQL_Workspace_
  derived_from _aterm:Saved_GraphQL_Query_
  requires _Corpus_Reading_Skill_
  derived_from _aterm:Command_Invocation_Guide_
  derived_from _aterm:UI_Session_
  derived_from _aterm:Control_UI_
  derived_from _aterm:Markdown_Note_
  derived_from _aterm:Environment_
  derived_from _aterm:UI_Context_
  derived_from _aterm:Aterm_Basics_Guide_
  derived_from _aterm:Explorer_Control_Guidance_
  derived_from _aterm:Query_Selection_Guidance_
  derived_from _aterm:Command_Invocation_Guide_
  derived_from _aterm:UI_Session_
  derived_from _aterm:Control_UI_
  derived_from _aterm:Markdown_Note_
  derived_from _aterm:Explorer_Control_Guidance_
  derived_from _aterm:Explorer_Presentation_Procedure_
  derived_from _aterm:UI_Context_
  references _Runtime_Management_Skill_
.description
  Use when an Agent should show or explain corpus information in an already connected Aterm browser.
.body
  ## Select the intended Session

  ```sh
  aterm ui session list
  aterm ui session view SESSION
  ```

  - Obtain the intended server and browser Session, requested UI outcome and exact qualified Term identities.
  - If the intended Home has no running server and the task requires starting it, use _Runtime_Management_Skill_ (“Select the environment before changing it” and “Run the intended service”). Then refresh Session discovery; starting a server does not create the intended browser Session.
  - Session UUIDs survive reconnects; a prefix of at least six characters must identify one Session uniquely.
  - Inspect location, readiness and visibility; last activity and heartbeat/last-seen time answer different questions.
  - Session ordering alone does not identify the user's intended browser.
  - If no intended connected Session is available, report unavailable and offer a useful alternative such as a Markdown explanation. Do not claim UI success, open another browser or choose a different Session silently.
  - Resolve exact qualified Terms before sending UI commands; these commands do not use selector globs or --knowledge to resolve ambiguity.

  ## Choose the state to change

  | Command | Effect |
  |---|---|
  | ui open TERM --session SESSION | Replace the Working Set with one Term and show Term Declarations with Context. |
  | ui explore set TERM... --session SESSION | Replace the Working Set and arrange/fit it in Explore. |
  | ui explore add TERM... --session SESSION | Add Terms; retain surviving positions and camera. |
  | ui explore remove TERM... --session SESSION | Remove Terms; retain surviving positions and camera. |
  | ui explore clear --session SESSION | Empty the Working Set; keep Context and camera. |
  | ui note send --session SESSION --file NOTE.md | Cache the Markdown Note and open Note; preserve the main view. |

  - Term Declarations, References and Graph Explore share an ordered Working Set; Structure shows the corpus hierarchy.
  - Tree Term-name clicks replace membership; checkboxes toggle it; Knowledge/Group checkboxes include matching collapsed descendants.
  - Reader links open Context; dragging a Term into the main view adds membership.
  - Working Set, Context, Note and graph Selection are independent states; choose commands by the requested effect.

  > Send a Note and claim the graph's Working Set changed — BAD.
  >
  > Apply the Explore set, confirm it, then send the explanation Note — GOOD.

  ```sh
  aterm ui explore set _vending_machine:Slot_ _vending_machine:Product_ --session SESSION
  aterm ui note send --session SESSION --file explanation.md
  ```

  ## Reuse a Query in the browser

  - For an interactive query task, open GraphQL in the intended browser's application menu; its route is /queries.
  - This section describes browser controls. The ui command vocabulary above does not open the Query editor, save Queries or run them.
  - Use the prerequisite Corpus Reading Skill's “Pass reusable parameters” section for variables and bindings.
  - Choose Trip date and related Terms, then set $knowledge and $date under Parameters.

  > $knowledge = japan_trip; $date = 2026-10-28 — finds matching structured scalar dates and directly connected Terms.

  - Inspect actual section values first; the example matches exact values, not dates inferred from names or prose, and does not compute recursive closure.
  - A parameter checkbox supplies or omits that variable; Variables JSON handles null, lists and objects. Select an operation name for a multi-operation document.
  - Run query executes the current values; changing parameters invalidates results. Cancel prevents a late response from replacing the current view.
  - Name and Save a reusable Query. Reload it from Saved queries; Save a copy creates a separate preset. Editing the name and saving renames it; deletion requires confirmation.
  - Saving or loading does not run the Query. Unsaved edits require an explicit discard when switching documents.
  - Saved Queries belong to this browser origin and Home; they do not synchronize to another device or become corpus Terms.
  - If storage fails or another tab changed the saved record, retain the draft and resolve the failure or save a copy; do not claim it was saved.
  - Include canonical Term id fields in the response to enable View Terms or Explore. Both replace Working Set membership; View Terms opens Term Declarations and Explore opens the returned Terms in Explore.

  > Click a result Term to read Context; choose View Terms to compare the returned Terms together.

  - Inspect errors and pageInfo before presenting results; when more pages remain, supply the corresponding endCursor parameter and rerun. Each run represents its returned pages, not an accumulated complete set.
  - Returning to Explorer preserves the Working Set; returning to GraphQL within the same browser session retains the editor draft. Save before closing the browser if the Query must be reusable.

  ## Confirm the supported outcome

  1. Send the command and retain its command UUID and acknowledgement.
  2. For an unknown outcome, inspect ui command view before retrying.
  3. Reuse --command-id with the same payload to avoid duplicate delivery; do not reuse that ID for a different command.
  4. After expiration or server restart, compare current state before creating a fresh command ID.
  5. Treat rejected, failed, disconnected and unavailable outcomes explicitly; an HTTP success alone is not proof that the browser applied the command.
  6. Compare the acknowledged guarantees and observable final state with every requested UI effect; report unconfirmed when evidence cannot establish it.

  - Explore commands activate Explore, preserve Context and support Undo; they do not add neighbors automatically.
  - Check the ordered terms and view/mode separately from term, the independent Context target. A Session UUID does not persist graph geometry.
  - After a busy or disconnected outcome, inspect readiness before another request.
  - An applied Note acknowledgement confirms caching and the reader update; it does not prove an asynchronous Mermaid diagram has finished rendering.
  - Note text uses --file or stdin; qualified Term tokens become links and unqualified tokens stay literal. Notes do not update the corpus.
  - Notes retain up to ten items in browser local storage for that origin and Home. Reading an older Note does not restore its previous graph.
  - After presenting a graph, send a concise Markdown explanation when requested; preserve the selected browser's independent state.
.reminder
  - When presentation is unavailable, offer an explicit alternative without claiming UI success.
  - Select the intended connected Session by identity and readiness, not ordering alone.
  - Resolve qualified Terms; distinguish Working Set, Context, Note and graph Selection.
  - Retain command IDs; inspect unknown outcomes before retrying the same payload.
  - Saved Queries are browser/Home-local; inspect errors and remaining pages before replacing the Working Set.
  - Verify acknowledged and observable effects; applied Note does not prove Mermaid finished rendering.
}
